    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Field on the Rigidbody2D</th><th>What it actually does</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Body Type</code></td>
            <td>Dynamic bodies fall and are pushed. Kinematic bodies move only where code puts them, and <strong>ignore gravity, forces and mass entirely</strong> — if your character stands still in mid-air with Gravity Scale 5, look here first.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Gravity Scale</code></td>
            <td>A <strong>multiplier</strong> on the project-wide gravity (Project Settings, Physics 2D, Y at <code>-9.81</code>). 0 is off, 1 is undiluted. Means nothing on a Kinematic body.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Mass</code></td>
            <td>How much the <em>same</em> force moves you. Gravity pulls harder on a heavy body, so mass cancels out in a fall — but a force you apply by hand divides by mass.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Constraints</code></td>
            <td>Freeze Rotation Z, set in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> so the character stops tumbling. It matters in the air too: an unfrozen body spins when it clips a corner mid-jump.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>What it requires</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse)</code></td>
            <td>A Dynamic body and Gravity Scale above 0.</td>
            <td>Mass divides into it, so retuning mass silently retunes every jump. Clean parabola — the row the walkthrough builds.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>linearVelocity = new Vector2(x, jumpSpeed)</code></td>
            <td>A Dynamic body and Gravity Scale above 0.</td>
            <td>Identical height every time, to the pixel — and it erases whatever vertical motion was already there: a platform's lift, a knockback, a bounce.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AddForce(Vector2.up * thrust, ForceMode2D.Force)</code> every frame while held</td>
            <td>A Dynamic body; some rule that stops it — fuel, ceiling, timer.</td>
            <td>A rocket, not a jump. Height depends on how long you hold, and this is what you get by accident if you forget the force mode.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>MovePosition()</code> in steps</td>
            <td>A <strong>Kinematic</strong> body, moved in <code>FixedUpdate()</code>.</td>
            <td>It ignores gravity, so it never falls, and it only collides with Dynamic bodies — so no floor is solid to it, and no <code>OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D collision)</code> fires against those static colliders. The cave door runs on that callback and would go silent.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: how does your script know it is in the cave?</strong>
        Your movement code assumes a top-down world; the cave needs the opposite assumptions, and
        the script has to be told which world it woke up in. All four options below are used in
        real projects. The walkthrough builds (c) — the one the cave's leftover override says the
        project itself chose.
        <ul>
            <li><strong>(a) Ask the scene its name</strong> — <code>SceneManager.GetActiveScene().name</code> against <code>"Platformer"</code>. Readable; breaks silently if someone renames the scene.</li>
            <li><strong>(b) Ask the scene its build index</strong> — the cave is index 2. Survives a rename; lies when the build order changes. <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> already does this, so read it before you borrow the idea.</li>
            <li><strong>(c) A serialized bool, ticked per scene instance</strong> — visible in the Inspector without pressing Play. Costs a tick on every new Player somebody places.</li>
            <li><strong>(d) A component that only exists on the cave's Player</strong> — the component's presence <em>is</em> the flag. Costs one more file and one more lookup.</li>
            <li>Whatever you pick, reuse a scene-mode flag you already built this year. Two flags that both mean "which world am I in" is how a project starts rotting.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

